"_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-26T20:55:11Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30219781560", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "The Yuri A. Gagarin State Scientific Research and Testing Cosmonaut Training Center is a Russian facility that trains cosmonauts for their space missions. It is in Star City of Moscow Oblast, a name which may refer to the facility itself or to its grounds.", "newValue": "The Yuri A. Gagarin State Scientific Research and Testing Cosmonaut Training Center, established in 1960 in Star City, Russia, is a pivotal institution in the history of human space exploration. Named after the first human in space, Yuri Gagarin, this facility has been instrumental in training generations of Russian cosmonauts and has played a crucial role in the Soviet and later Russian space programs. Its influence extends beyond national boundaries, as it has contributed to international space collaborations and advancements in human spaceflight.

Since its inception, the center has trained over 1,800 cosmonauts, including notable figures such as Valentina Tereshkova, the first woman in space, and Alexei Arkhipovich, a celebrated space commander. The training provided at the center encompasses a wide range of skills, from spacecraft operation to survival training in various environments. The facility's rigorous programs have ensured that cosmonauts are well-prepared for the physical and psychological demands of space travel, significantly contributing to the success of missions such as the Apollo-Soyuz Test Project in 1975, which marked the first international human spaceflight mission.

